{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0161CbCjcWccG4ZTHYztgpgC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the task carefully before implementing.\n\n## Task Overview\nImplement `pkg/testutils` differential testing harness with:\n1. `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` - DiffTest struct and Run method\n2. `pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go` - Unit tests\n\n## Requirements Analysis\n\nFrom the requirements:\n- **R1**: DiffTest struct with specific fields\n- **R2**: Run method executing both refBin and goBin with identical inputs\n- **R3**: Compare Go binary output against want values, call t.Errorf for each mismatch\n- **R4**: Tests must exercise 3 specific cases\n\n\n- **D1**: File named difftest.go (not testutils.go)\n- **D2**: Use os/exec.Command, bytes.Buffer for stdout/stderr, *exec.ExitError for exit code\n- **D3**: Use t.Errorf (not t.Fatalf) - report all mismatches\n- **D4**: Standard library only, no pkg/sys, pkg/format, or cmd/ imports\n\nNow I'm setting up the implementation structure with the DiffTest struct, its Run method that executes both binaries with identical inputs, and then comparing the Go binary's output against expected values using t.Errorf for any discrepancies. Defining the struct fields...\n\nNow I'm implementing the Run method that executes both the reference and Go binaries, comparing their outputs against the expected values to surface any divergences in a single test run. Checking the exit code and comparing the normalized stdout and stderr against expected values, reporting any mismatches.\n\nLooking back at the requirements, I see that both binaries get executed with the same inputs, but the actual comparison is against the `Want*` fields rather than directly comparing the reference binary's output to the Go binary's output. The reference binary might be running for validation or to ensure the inputs are sound, but the real test is whether the Go binary produces the