n. I'm going in circles here. The key question is whether Want* fields are the source of truth or if refBin output is. R3 explicitly says to compare against Want*, so I'll stick with that interpretation—refBin runs but serves a secondary purpose rather than defining the expected values. I need to read the prd001-testutils.yaml file since it's listed as required reading but wasn't provided in the project context, and the constraints allow reading files not already given to me. This should clarify whether refBin is actually needed and how the comparison logic should work.\n\nNow I'm setting up the difftest.go package structure with the differential testing harness that will execute both the Go implementation and reference binary with identical inputs, then compare their outputs byte-for-byte.\n\nWriting the package implementation... Defining the test structure with fields for expected output and normalization functions, then implementing the Run method that executes both binaries and compares their results. Writing test assertions... I'm setting up the capture function to execute a binary with specified arguments, stdin, and environment variables, then collecting the exit code and output streams. The function handles both successful execution and exit errors, returning the appropriate exit code along with stdout and stderr.\n\nNow I'm moving on to write tests for the difftest functionality.\n\nI need to test three scenarios: when all outputs match, when the exit code differs, and when NormStdout's trailing newline stripping resolves a mismatch. I'll use a self-referencing test binary with TestMain and create a mock testing.T to verify that t.Errorf gets called in the right cases.\n\nWriting mock T implementation...\n\nBut I'm realizing the Run method takes a concrete `*testing.T`, not an interface, so I can't easily swap in a mock. Let me think about using `t.Run` with `t.Failed()` to detect whether the assertion actually failed instead. I'm realizing the signature is